{"causes": ["The early persecution of Muslims by the Quraysh in Mecca necessitated a clandestine meeting place for the nascent community.", "Prophet Muhammad's need for a secure and private location to teach new converts and conduct prayers away from public scrutiny.", "Al-Arqam's early conversion to Islam and his steadfast loyalty to Prophet Muhammad positioned him to offer his personal residence."], "effects": ["Dar al-Arqam provided a safe haven for the early Muslim community, facilitating the conversion of key figures like Umar ibn al-Khattab.", "The clandestine meetings in Al-Arqam's house allowed the foundational teachings of Islam to be disseminated and solidified among early adherents.", "Al-Arqam's dedication of his home became a lasting symbol of sacrifice and commitment in Islamic tradition, influencing later generations."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "prophet-muhammad", "sourceName": "Prophet Muhammad", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "al-arqam-ibn-abi-al-arqam", "targetName": "Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam", "context": "Prophet Muhammad collaborated with Al-Arqam by using his house, Dar al-Arqam, as a secret meeting place for early Muslims from 613-622 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "al-arqam-ibn-abi-al-arqam", "sourceName": "Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "dar-al-arqam", "targetName": "Dar al-Arqam", "context": "Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam was the owner of Dar al-Arqam, the pivotal house where early Muslims secretly gathered in Mecca."}, {"sourceSlug": "early-islamic-community", "sourceName": "Early Islamic Community", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "mecca-saudi-arabia", "targetName": "Mecca, Saudi Arabia", "context": "The early Islamic community, including Al-Arqam, primarily operated in Mecca, Saudi Arabia, during the initial phase of Islam."}, {"sourceSlug": "al-arqam-ibn-abi-al-arqam", "sourceName": "Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-badr", "targetName": "Battle of Badr", "context": "Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam participated in the Battle of Badr in 624 CE, demonstrating his commitment to the Muslim cause."}, {"sourceSlug": "quraysh", "sourceName": "Quraysh", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "al-arqam-ibn-abi-al-arqam", "targetName": "Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam", "context": "The persecution by the Quraysh clan influenced Al-Arqam to offer his house as a safe haven for the early Muslim community."}], "places": [{"name": "Mecca, Saudi Arabia", "role": "Primary residence and site of Dar al-Arqam"}, {"name": "Medina, Saudi Arabia", "role": "Post-Hijra residence and burial place"}, {"name": "Mount Safa, Saudi Arabia", "role": "Location near Dar al-Arqam"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "al-arqam-ibn-abi-al-arqam"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q287317", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Arqam_ibn_Abi_al-Arqam"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Al-Arqam's provision of Dar al-Arqam as a secret sanctuary for early Muslims was critical for the survival and initial growth of Islam, enabling the conversion of pivotal figures like Umar and laying the groundwork for a world-changing religion. His actions directly facilitated the clandestine phase of a faith that would profoundly reshape global history.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-22T11:09:56Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45015769184", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Al-Arqam ibn Abī al-Arqam was an early companion of Prophet Muhammad. He was the owner of the house where the early Muslim community held its meetings.", "newValue": "Al-Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam (died 673 CE) was a pivotal figure in the nascent Islamic community in Mecca during the early 7th century, serving as an early companion and confidant of Prophet Muhammad. His significance is intrinsically linked to his residence, Dar al-Arqam, which clandestinely hosted the first Muslims for over a decade, providing a sanctuary for prayer, instruction, and conversion during a period of intense persecution by the Quraysh polytheists. This house became the clandestine nucleus of a world-changing movement, making Al-Arqam a foundational, albeit often unsung, patron of early Islam. He was among the first ten individuals to embrace Islam, a testament to his unwavering conviction and proximity to the Prophet.

From approximately 613 CE to 622 CE, Dar al-Arqam, located at the foot of Mount Safa in Mecca, functioned as the secret headquarters for Muhammad's mission. Within its walls, many prominent figures, including Umar ibn al-Khattab and Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib, converted to Islam, receiving direct instruction from the Prophet away from the hostile gaze of the Meccan elite. Al-Arqam's willingness to dedicate his home, a place of personal safety and privacy, to the nascent faith at great personal risk, underscored his profound commitment and facilitated the foundational growth of the Muslim community before the Hijra.

Following the migration to Medina in 622 CE, Al-Arqam continued to be an active participant in the Muslim community, fighting in key battles such as Badr, Uhud, and the Trench, demonstrating his commitment beyond the confines of his Meccan home. His house, Dar al-Arqam, eventually passed to his son, then his grandson, and later became a waqf (endowment) for charitable purposes, preserving its memory as a sacred site of early Islamic history. His legacy is not tied to grand political power but to the quiet, foundational support that enabled a persecuted minority to survive and eventually thrive."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-22T11:09:56Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45015769184", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "590 CE, Mecca"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "590 CE, Mecca"}], "newValue": ["The early persecution of Muslims by the Quraysh in Mecca necessitated a clandestine meeting place for the nascent community.", "Prophet Muhammad's need for a secure and private location to teach new converts and conduct prayers away from public scrutiny.", "Al-Arqam's early conversion to Islam and his steadfast loyalty to Prophet Muhammad positioned him to offer his personal residence."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-22T11:09:56Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45015769184", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "673 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "673 CE"}], "newValue": ["Dar al-Arqam provided a safe haven for the early Muslim community, facilitating the conversion of key figures like Umar ibn al-Khattab.", "The clandestine meetings in Al-Arqam's house all